<!--
  kuna: a call whose fall-through is the function's own literal pool does not return.
  `probe` in the ARM32 fixtures (little- and big-endian, source poolfallthru.c) ends
  `bl use; bl die` with no epilogue, and its five PC-relative literal words follow the
  second call. kuna cannot prove `die` no-return, so the walk took the call's
  fall-through, decoded the first four words as instructions and stopped at
  `0x0002cbb2`, which decodes as nothing, while `kuna disassemble` lists the same
  words as data.
  Stage: P2 flow classification, the funcboundflow fall-through bound
  (`p2_lift/kuna_funcboundflow.rs` kuna_should_bound_at_pool) over the pool extents
  the Listing proves from trusted references (`kuna-analysis`
  `listing/kuna_poolextents.rs`), so `option listing on` precedes `read symbols`.
  P0 assertion: `option funcboundflow off|on` (default on).
  `poolguards.s` drives every condition in both byte orders: eight negatives
  violating exactly one each (conditional call, branch-targeted word, halfword read,
  live gap, over-wide gap, word in a writable executable section, a callee that
  provably returns with the pool's only load past a call, a reader past a call
  nothing proves returns), `svc_junk` (a data
  word past a non-returning `svc` that spells a load of the returning call's
  fall-through: `return 5` stays and both passes agree), and seven positives
  (`bounded_call`, `pad_call` with one instruction of padding, `indirect_call` through
  `blx`, `two_pools` with two bounded calls, `wide_literal` through `vldr`,
  `ordered_loads` with its load after the call, `junk_pointer` whose pool word spells
  a load of live code that stays).
  Assert 1 counts the ten `Bad instruction` truncations: the off pass of `probe` in
  each byte order, the off pass of `svc_junk` in each byte order (two each: without
  the bound the junk after `svc` runs on through the functions that follow), and per
  byte order `live_gap` and `data_word`, whose fall-throughs are correctly not
  bounded and so still decode their pools. Assert 2 counts the eighteen bounded
  calls: the default pass of `probe` in each byte order and eight per byte order
  (two in `two_pools`). Assert 3 pins the two indirect ones and assert 4 the six
  `return 5` of `svc_junk`: its guard print and both passes, in both byte orders.
-->
